everything old is new again and i'm not  exactly thrilled by that  usually i'd put more time between doing  episodes of animation junkie but this is  something i've been meaning to talk  about for a little while now and it's  really  gotten on hand lately and i need to talk  about this and that is  disney remaking their animated features  i've mentioned somewhat offhandedly in  other videos that i am not a big fan  of the idea of taking an animated film  and remaking it  as a live-action movie i don't have a  real substantive argument to make  and that at least that would be any  different from the argument you'd make  against remaking anything again so  really in a lot of ways what it comes  down to is i feel that that  inherently belittles the medium of  animation  i remember back when the first lord of  the rings movies were coming out and the  anticipation was really building and i  was in  an online board and somebody asked the  question why are they starting with the  lord of the rings why wouldn't you do  the hobbit first and one of the things  that i  threw out there is possibly being a  factor was that we already had a  perfectly  serviceable version of the hobbit and  that is the rank and bass animated  version  and pretty much the immediate response  that i got from people was like  yeah that's all fine but i'm talking  about a real movie  and that was really kind of the first  time that it hammered into me that  my notion of holding animation at the  same level  as live action that i do most people  don't now i'm really kind of singling  out disney here because what they're  doing right now specifically  has me really really irritated and if  you haven't been following they  obviously have been doing somewhat  recently a number of  live-action i'm not going to call them  spin-offs but i'll say reinterpretations  of stuff that they had  i suppose you could say it kind of  kicked off with tim burton's alice in  wonderland  there was maleficent and then earlier  this year we had cinderella now it's  really  starting with cinderella that i start to  get annoyed because  alice in wonderland and maleficent while  not necessarily great movies i  understand  why they were made in there you can make  an artistic argument for why they exist  in the case of alice in wonderland they  technically it was a sequel  although it was a weird kind of sequel  where she didn't remember being in  wonderland before the first time so i i  don't know what that was about but  regardless of you have tim burton make  alice in wonderland  and you just kind of go well why  wouldn't you do that that's like all in  his  wheelhouse and i suppose by making it a  sequel instead of a direct adaptation  gave  him the freedom to go you know in a  notably different place than the  original book itself or even  the previous disney animation went i  don't think it worked but he  was allowed that freedom with maleficent  this is obviously a very different  retelling from a different character's  perspective so it's a notably different  story again  did quite work started out strongly lost  steam towards the end but this is not a  review of those movies  in the case of cinderella they pretty  much just did  their animated version again in live  action  now i have to be honest i have not seen  that new version of cinderella i don't  intend to see it but  pretty much all the response has been  very positive people said it was a very  good movie that was better than  maleficent it was better than  alice in wonderland and i i will believe  that i'm not  doubting that but i don't care if it's  better than those  if all they're doing is taking the  animation and doing it again in live  action i understand it's not literally  the same there were minor tweaks to it  cinderella and the prince meet  before the ball once so you know it's  not completely  ridiculous that they fall for each other  on the first view  structurally it's the same movie and i  i've done my research i've done this  i've read the synopsis i've read the  characters it is the same thing with  very very minor tweaks and i just feel  that inherently belittles  not only the original but animation as  an art form because now  people go okay well that's the that's  the kitty version here's the real  version but it's the same  thing and i understand that these are  all adaptations these are not original  stories they weren't the animated film  first these came from source material  and there is a validity  to reinterpreting that source material  to go back to it  and stay true to it take it in a  different direction emphasize a  different aspect of it that was maybe  dropped from the previous film  whatever but that's not what they're  doing that's not what they did with  cinderella and it's not what they appear  to be doing in all the newly announced  features and i can't even remember what  all of them  are anymore but the ones that i can  think of off the top my head there's  beauty and the beast which they've got  mostly cast at this point  they've got the jungle book which i  think comes out next year and they've  since announced  dumbo and winnie the pooh now granted  we're really far away from those ones  but  you cannot tell me that there's any way  they're going to be able to do those  live action without doing  the character of dumbo and the character  of winnie the pooh and all of his  friends  as cgi and as far as i'm concerned cgi  is just animation again so what are you  doing and i think really what set  everything off in the wrong course was  the fact that cinderella succeeded and  it succeeded  with critics and also with audiences  it's done well at the box office  maleficent did decent at the box office  but critics were very divided it wasn't  a  universal acclaim and that was when they  went in a different direction with it  then they came out with the film that  very much stuck to what they did before  and did much better and now it looks  like they're just gonna do that  and this isn't just me speculating on  this because you look at what they've  done and what they've announced  in terms of these films beauty and the  beast is  to be a musical again they haven't  necessarily confirmed they're using the  exact same songs again but  they announced that josh gab is going to  be lefou now lefou  does not come from any version of the  original story of beauty and the beast  lefou is a creation of disney so they  are  not retelling the story of beauty and  the beast they are remaking  their film and the jungle book is  another one because  if there is any story that disney has  done an adaptation of  that you go back to the source material  and there are a million different things  different ways so much stuff that they  dropped so many themes they did not work  with  it's the jungle book there's a lot of  different ways that you could do that  but every indication is that they're  doing it the same to the point that  baloo  is still gonna sing bear necessities  which is iconic not to the story  but to the animated film and i think i  know why  disney is doing this because they can't  do what they used to do  because what they used to do was  re-release the animated films back into  theaters  now for those of you who are on the  younger side are used to a really packed  movie schedule where there's  at least two new movies out every single  weekend that is a pretty recent trend  that was not the norm for a very long  time and there used to be  gaps weeks would go by and there would  be no new films so  older films would get re-released to  theaters and disney  was really the master at this i got to  see things like  101 dalmatians fantasia and peter pan  in the theater despite the fact that all  those movies are older than i am  but they can't do that anymore not only  because there isn't really a gap and an  opening to put in a movie that's already  come out but also because of home video  because the reason they were so  successful doing that is that  home video wasn't really a thing yet  even getting into the late 80s you know  people had vhs's but you they were still  mostly going to rent them not a lot of  people own them now  home media people own these movies they  can watch them whenever they want and i  think that's why  they've kind of eased off the  re-releasing of their animated films in  3d which was the closest they were kind  of coming to that old thing but  i don't think they were seeing the  returns they wanted for that what their  so what they've done instead is  how can we milk more money out of these  things we've already done  well we can remake it again in live  action we don't have to  change anything we'll just do it with  actors and we'll  make all the money and that just drives  me crazy because like i said  not only are they just sort of strip  mining these classics and they are  classics most of these are  classic movies but they're not going  back to the original versions of the  story and coming up with a new  interpretation they are strip mining  their  own stuff and as a lover of animation  that  just inherently belittles what  they did and what they did with these  animated films  is amazing and i don't think there's  anything that we can do to stop this and  i'm not even gonna make the step to say  don't go see these movies because you  know what  the movies will probably be good like i  said every indication was that  cinderella was good  and it probably is but its existence  just  aggravates me on so many levels as a  lover of these movies and as a lover of  animation in general  i just hate this  i hate this trend and i hate that one of  the  giants of animation is leading the way  in belittling the art form that made  them what they are today  and i don't really have much more of a  capper to put on this  i'm just depressed so what about you  what are your thoughts on all of this do  you care do you not was it even on your  radar that this sort of thing was going  on  whatever the case is drop something down  in the comments and let's talk about it  as for me  i'll be back as soon as i can get my  next fix

